Lazy keys unique per ForEach block and section: SkipUI keys a lazy ForEach row by its raw ID and section chrome by the enclosing ForEach tag, so an ID repeated across sections, an ID repeated within one ForEach, or one ForEach element yielding two Sections throws Compose's "Key ... was already used" once those rows near the visible range, where SwiftUI accepts all three; LazyItemCollector now builds each row key as <block>|<escaped id>#<occurrence> from a ForEach identity remembered where the ForEach is evaluated, counts occurrences in data order so a key is the same however Compose asks for it, keys sections by a remembered Section identity with #n for repeats, evaluates lazy ForEach elements in groups keyed by identity so nested identities follow a reorder, and leaves scrollTo on the original IDs (List, LazyVStack, LazyHStack, the lazy grids, Table).

